@startuml BDD Structure - Commission Calculator with JBehave

' Define the styles (matching existing patterns)
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam classBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam arrowColor #6c757d
skinparam stereotypeCBackgroundColor #e9ecef
skinparam noteBorderColor #6c757d
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #fff3cd

' Title
title Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) with JBehave

' Story Files
class "commission_calculation.story" as Story1 <<StoryFile>> {
  Narrative: As a sales manager...
  --
  Scenario: Calculate basic commission
  Scenario: Tiered commission rates
  Scenario: Apply performance bonus
  Scenario: Full commission pipeline
}

class "deal_validation.story" as Story2 <<StoryFile>> {
  Narrative: As a system admin...
  --
  Scenario: Validate required fields
  Scenario: Validate positive value
  Scenario: Validate close date
  Scenario: Boundary testing
}

class "commission_plan.story" as Story3 <<StoryFile>> {
  Narrative: As a sales ops manager...
  --
  Scenario: Create commission plan
  Scenario: Activate commission plan
  Scenario: Check plan applicability
  Scenario: Add commission tiers
}

' Step Definitions
class CommissionCalculationSteps <<StepsDefinition>> {
  -commissionService: BDD
  -currentDeal: Deal
  -calculatedCommission: BigDecimal
  --
  <b>@Given Steps (Setup)</b>
  +givenSalesRepresentative(name, id)
  +givenDealWithValue(value, product)
  +givenDealStatus(status)
  +givenDealClosedDaysAgo(days)
  +givenCommissionPlan(planName)
  --
  <b>@When Steps (Actions)</b>
  +whenCalculateCommission(rate)
  +whenApplyBonus(bonus)
  +whenProcessDeals(table)
  +whenValidateDeal()
  +whenActivatePlan()
  --
  <b>@Then Steps (Verification)</b>
  +thenCommissionShouldBe(amount)
  +thenSystemShouldReject()
  +thenValidationShouldFail()
  +thenPlanStatusShouldBe(status)
}

' BDD Service Implementation
class BDD <<Implementation>> {
  -calculationCount: int
  -commissionPlans: Map<String, Plan>
  --
  <b>Commission Calculation</b>
  +calculateBasicCommission(value, rate): BigDecimal
  +calculateTieredCommission(value): BigDecimal
  +calculateCommissionWithBonus(base, bonus): BigDecimal
  +calculateFullCommission(deal, bonus): BigDecimal
  --
  <b>Validation (Business Rules)</b>
  +isDealEligibleForCommission(deal): boolean
  +getIneligibilityReason(deal): String
  --
  <b>Commission Plan Management</b>
  +storeCommissionPlan(plan): void
  +activateCommissionPlan(name): void
  +isPlanApplicableOn(name, date): boolean
  +getCommissionPlan(name): CommissionPlan
  --
  <b>Observability</b>
  +getCalculationCount(): int
  +resetCalculationCount(): void
  +reset(): void
}

' Domain Models
class Deal <<DomainModel>> {
  -id: String
  -title: String
  -value: BigDecimal
  -status: DealStatus
  -salesRepId: String
  -closeDate: LocalDate
  +getValue(): BigDecimal
  +getStatus(): DealStatus
  +getCloseDate(): LocalDate
}

class CommissionPlan <<DomainModel>> {
  -id: String
  -name: String
  -status: PlanStatus
  -effectiveStartDate: LocalDate
  -effectiveEndDate: LocalDate
  +isActiveOn(date): boolean
}

enum DealStatus <<Enum>> {
  OPEN
  CLOSED
  LOST
  PENDING
}

enum PlanStatus <<Enum>> {
  DRAFT
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}

' JBehave Runner
class CommissionCalculationStoriesTest <<TestRunner>> {
  +configuration(): Configuration
  +stepsFactory(): InjectableStepsFactory
  --
  Configures JBehave framework
  Loads story files
  Maps steps to Java classes
  Generates reports
}

' JBehave Framework
class "JBehave Framework" as JBehave <<Framework>> {
  <b>Core Components:</b>
  • Story Loader
  • Step Matcher
  • Report Generator
  • Table Parser
}

' Relationships
Story1 -down-> CommissionCalculationSteps : mapped to
Story2 -down-> CommissionCalculationSteps : mapped to
Story3 -down-> CommissionCalculationSteps : mapped to

CommissionCalculationSteps -right-> BDD : uses
BDD -down-> Deal : validates & calculates
BDD -down-> CommissionPlan : manages
Deal --> DealStatus : has
CommissionPlan --> PlanStatus : has

CommissionCalculationStoriesTest -up-> Story1 : runs
CommissionCalculationStoriesTest -up-> Story2 : runs
CommissionCalculationStoriesTest -up-> Story3 : runs
CommissionCalculationStoriesTest --> CommissionCalculationSteps : provides
CommissionCalculationStoriesTest ..> JBehave : uses

' Component Notes

note top of Story1
  <b>Story Files (Gherkin)</b>

  Written in natural language using
  Given-When-Then format.

  <b>Structure:</b>
  • Narrative: User story
  • Scenarios: Concrete examples
  • Steps: Given/When/Then

  <b>Example:</b>
  Given a deal worth $100,000
  When I calculate commission at 10%
  Then commission should be $10,000

  <b>Purpose:</b>
  Executable specifications readable
  by business stakeholders.
end note

note bottom of CommissionCalculationSteps
  <b>Step Definitions (Glue Code)</b>

  Maps Gherkin steps to Java code using
  JBehave annotations.

  <b>Key Annotations:</b>
  • @Given: Setup context
  • @When: Perform action
  • @Then: Verify outcome
  • @BeforeScenario: Initialize
  • @AfterScenario: Cleanup

  <b>Features:</b>
  • Parameter extraction: $variable
  • Table support: ExamplesTable
  • Regular expressions
  • Scenario state management

  <b>Purpose:</b>
  Bridge between natural language
  and implementation.
end note

note right of BDD
  <b>BDD Implementation</b>

  Business logic implementing
  scenarios from story files.

  <b>BDD Principles:</b>
  • Ubiquitous language
  • Business-focused methods
  • Clear error messages
  • Testable business rules
  • Scenario independence

  <b>Business Rules:</b>
  • Only CLOSED deals eligible
  • 90-day eligibility window
  • Tiered rates: 8%, 10%, 12%
  • Performance bonuses

  <b>Purpose:</b>
  Implement behavior described
  in scenarios.
end note

note left of Deal
  <b>Domain Models</b>

  Business entities used in
  BDD scenarios.

  Models represent real-world
  concepts that business
  stakeholders understand:
  • Deals
  • Commission Plans
  • Sales Representatives

  <b>BDD Principle:</b>
  Use domain terminology
  that everyone understands.
end note

note bottom of CommissionCalculationStoriesTest
  <b>JBehave Runner</b>

  JUnit test class that:
  • Configures JBehave
  • Finds story files
  • Maps steps to classes
  • Runs scenarios
  • Generates reports

  <b>Execution:</b>
  mvn test -Dtest=CommissionCalculationStoriesTest

  <b>Reports Generated:</b>
  • Console output
  • HTML reports
  • XML for CI/CD
end note

note right of JBehave
  <b>JBehave Framework</b>

  BDD framework for Java that:
  • Parses Gherkin syntax
  • Matches steps to methods
  • Executes scenarios
  • Generates reports

  <b>Key Features:</b>
  • Story file support (.story)
  • Annotation-based steps
  • Parameter extraction
  • Table-driven tests
  • Lifecycle hooks
  • Multiple report formats

  <b>Benefits:</b>
  • Natural language specs
  • Stakeholder collaboration
  • Living documentation
  • Automated testing
end note

' BDD Workflow
note as BDDWorkflow
  <b>BDD Workflow: Discovery → Formulation → Automation</b>

  <b>1. DISCOVERY (Collaborative)</b>
  Team discusses feature with examples
  Product owner, developers, QA collaborate
  Identify scenarios and edge cases
  → Result: Shared understanding

  <b>2. FORMULATION (Write Scenarios)</b>
  Convert examples to Gherkin format
  Write .story files with Given-When-Then
  Review scenarios with stakeholders
  → Result: Executable specifications

  <b>3. AUTOMATION (Implement Steps)</b>
  Create @Given/@When/@Then methods
  Implement business logic in BDD class
  Run scenarios to verify behavior
  → Result: Working feature + tests

  <b>4. LIVING DOCUMENTATION</b>
  Scenarios run in CI/CD pipeline
  Reports show current behavior
  Documentation stays up-to-date
  → Result: Continuous verification
end note

' BDD vs TDD
note as Comparison
  <b>BDD vs TDD Comparison</b>

  <b>TDD (Test-Driven Development):</b>
  • Developer-focused
  • Technical test code
  • Implementation details
  • Example: testCalculate()

  <b>BDD (Behavior-Driven Development):</b>
  • Stakeholder-focused
  • Natural language scenarios
  • Business behavior
  • Example: Given-When-Then

  <b>When to Use Each:</b>
  • TDD: Low-level algorithms, utilities
  • BDD: Business features, workflows

  <b>Best Practice:</b>
  Use both! BDD for features,
  TDD for implementation.
end note

' Key Benefits
note as Benefits
  <b>BDD Benefits</b>

  ✅ <b>Shared Understanding</b>
     Everyone speaks same language

  ✅ <b>Clearer Requirements</b>
     Examples eliminate ambiguity

  ✅ <b>Living Documentation</b>
     Specs that stay up-to-date

  ✅ <b>Better Collaboration</b>
     Bridge business and tech

  ✅ <b>Reduced Waste</b>
     Build right thing first time

  ✅ <b>Executable Specifications</b>
     Requirements that can be tested

  ✅ <b>Business Value Focus</b>
     Test what matters to users

  ✅ <b>Confidence</b>
     Know system does what it should
end note

